const LINES={
 olsen:{t:"Olsen & Stub",sub:"Lofoten & Ålesund",img:"lofoten",txt:"The father's line runs from the fishing village of Kabelvåg in Lofoten to Bergen. Anton Martin Olsen, born at Følstadvik in Vågan in 1868, married Elise Marie Stub, born in Ålesund, in 1896. He died in 1904 and left her a widow with three children. She later moved to Bergen with her sons, where Arne Robert became a railway station worker. The Stub family goes back through Johan Jakob Britanus Stub, a steamship stoker, to the Ålesund baker Kristofer Abelsen Stub (born 1809 at Fyllingen), whose parents were the merchant Jørgen (Johan) Daniel Stub and Christine Margrethe Abelsæt (c. 1778–1819). Trygve Johan's double name, Stub-Olsen, kept his mother's surname.",ids:["arne46","arne99","ingeborgO","anton","elise","trygve","aslaug","martinus","johan","karen","kristofer","johanneJ","jorgen","christine","dorthea","hansG"]},
 hatlem:{t:"Hatlem",story:{id:"america",t:"The Hatlem family in America",paras:[["Two generations of Hatlem men crossed the Atlantic. On 29 May 1890 a Hans Hansen Hatlem, born 1868 in Hyllestad and an unmarried farmer's son, registered with the Bergen police to emigrate. The name, year and parish all match Gaute's great-grandfather Hans Hatlem, so this is very likely him. Great-grandfather Hans Hatlem, aged 21, arrived in New York on the Gallia on 13 June 1890, bound for Wisconsin; Gaute has confirmed his name on the FamilySearch passenger record.",["em1890","fh"]],["Ingeborg Hatlem's younger brother Hans Gerhard Smilden left Bergen in May 1893 at the age of 19. After some years in the Minnesota lumber camps he took a homestead near Larimore in Grand Forks County, North Dakota, in 1896, and married Maren Totland. They had ten children. Hans died there in 1928. That makes the North Dakota Smildens close cousins of the Hatlems. When Hans Hatlem and his son Hjalmar travelled to North Dakota in 1912, they may well have been visiting Hans Gerhard, Ingeborg's brother, though no record says so.",["em1893hg","nhs","obtena"]],["Two of his siblings settled in America for good, according to US records quoted on the Nasjonalarkivet forum (marked probable). His brother Andreas, later Andrew, registered in Bergen on 26 August 1898, arrived in Philadelphia that September and built a life in Minneapolis as a steamfitter and telephone company worker. He died in Minnesota in 1962. His sister Anne Marie, born 1884 and a seamstress in Bergen, left in April 1907 for Wisconsin. She was probably the Mary Hatlem working as a servant in Minneapolis in 1910. Around 1910 she married Harry Myron Hornbeck and became Marie Hornbeck, raising a son and a daughter in Oakland and later San Francisco. She died in South San Francisco in 1974. Andrew married Annie C. Christianson. Their daughter Florence was born in Minneapolis in 1922, and Annie died in 1946.",["em1898","em1907","fh","fh2","sbn"]],["In 1894 Hans married Ingeborg Hansdatter of Smilden. On 22 August 1912, by then a married farmer living at Myrdal in Fana, he registered to emigrate again, this time to North Dakota and taking his son Hjalmar (born 1898). Both travelled with the Cunard Line. Father and son sailed on the Ascania to Quebec and crossed into the US through the St. Albans, Vermont district in September 1912. Hans's border card records that he had lived at Marinette, Wisconsin, in 1890–1892, and that they were heading for Larimore, North Dakota, to Ingeborg's brother Hans Gerhard Smilden. Their stay was short: by the 1920 census both father and son were back in Norway, living at Valberg in Nesttun. Hjalmar was then working as a building carpenter.",["em1912","card12","hh1920"]],["Their son Harald, Gaute's grandfather, followed in 1925. He was a 21-year-old typographer from Nesttun when he registered in Bergen on 13 July, giving Minneapolis, Minnesota as his destination. His ticket had been paid for and sent from America. That suggests someone there helped him, possibly relatives, but no record says who it was. One possibility is his uncle Andrew Hatlem, who lived in Minneapolis, but this is only a guess.",["em1925"]],["He left England on 18 July 1925 and arrived in New York on the Aquitania on 24 July.",["uk1925","di1928"]],["By 1928 he was living in Chicago, not Minneapolis. On 12 May 1928, as Harold Hatlem, a 23-year-old printer living at 915 E. 42nd Place, he filed a Declaration of Intention to become a US citizen. He was described as fair, blond and blue-eyed, 5 ft 6 in tall, and signed as Harold H. Hatlem.",["di1928"]],["The family knows that Harald came home to Norway after a few years. The exact date after 1928 has not been found. In 1944 he married Klara Cecilie Henneli in Fana.",["m1944"]]],img:["img/harald-declaration-1928.jpg","Harald's Declaration of Intention, Chicago, 12 May 1928. Source: FamilySearch, found by Gaute"]},sub:"Hyllestad, Sogn",img:"sogn",txt:"Hatlem lies by the fjord in Øn parish, Hyllestad, in Sogn. There are two farms, Indre and Ytre Hatlem, and this family farmed Ytre Hatlem (gnr. 27). Hans Hanssen (born 1838) and Petrine Andersdatter (born 1842) raised their children there. Their son Hans (born 1868) took over the farm before 1900, then moved with his family to Vestfold and later to Nesttun near Bergen. His son Harald became a typographer in Bergen.",ids:["harald","hansj","ingeborgH","hanss","petrine","andrew","annemarie","anders","trine","vanH","florence","petrineH","henninge","hjalmar","herman","arthur","hansH"]},
 henne:{t:"Henne & Nøttveit",sub:"Fana, Bergen",img:"fana",txt:"Klara Cecilie was born in 1914 on the farm Henne in Fana, south of Bergen, and baptised in Birkeland church. Her parents were Lars Larsen Hennelien, a farmer at Henne, and Marta Paulsdatter, whose family farm was Nøttveit. Klara Cecilie had at least eight brothers and sisters, and the family used the farm name Henneli.",ids:["klara","lars","marta"]},
};
